CROSS-BROWSER AND ASSISTIVE-TECHNOLOGY COVERAGE (R29) — audit host, 2026-09-03
Every line below is a command that was run on the audit host and its real output.

── Chromium: EXERCISED ────────────────────────────────────────────────
$ ls ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright
chromium-1234
chromium_headless_shell-1234
ffmpeg-1011

Every browser probe in this audit drives that Chromium over CDP
(scripts/lib/probe-browser.mjs). It is the only engine present on this host.

── Firefox: ENVIRONMENTAL (absent, three ways) ────────────────────────
$ ls -d /Applications/Firefox.app
ls: /Applications/Firefox.app: No such file or directory
$ which firefox geckodriver
firefox not found
geckodriver not found
$ ls ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright | grep -i firefox
(no output)

No Firefox engine, no geckodriver, and no Playwright Firefox download. Nothing
was measured in Gecko, so nothing about Gecko is claimed.

── WebKit: NOT EXERCISED, and the refusal is recorded ─────────────────
$ ls -d /Applications/Safari.app /System/Cryptexes/App/usr/bin/safaridriver
/Applications/Safari.app
/System/Cryptexes/App/usr/bin/safaridriver

The engine and its WebDriver server are both present, so this is not absence —
it is a machine-level permission the audit cannot grant itself. Measured rather
than assumed:

$ /System/Cryptexes/App/usr/bin/safaridriver -p 4470 &
$ curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4470/session -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
       -d '{"capabilities":{"alwaysMatch":{"browserName":"safari"}}}'
{"value":{"error":"session not created","message":"Could not create a session:
You must enable 'Allow remote automation' in the Developer section of Safari
Settings to control Safari via WebDriver.","stacktrace":""}}

Enabling it is a human action in Safari Settings (plus `safaridriver --enable`,
which prompts for administrator authorization), and no WebDriver client is a
dependency of this repository:

$ node -e '...Object.keys({...dependencies,...devDependencies}).filter(/playwright|puppeteer|selenium|webdriver|cypress/)'
no webdriver/playwright/puppeteer/selenium dependency

── The one engine-gated API in the product ────────────────────────────
`EyeDropper` is Chromium-only and is the single browser API this product uses
that a supported browser may not have. It is feature-detected, not assumed:

  components/editor/color/ColorPopoverPanel.tsx:84-85
    const eyedropperAvailable =
      typeof window !== "undefined" && "EyeDropper" in window;
  components/editor/color/ColorPopoverPanel.tsx:165
    {eyedropperAvailable ? ( ...the screen-picker button... ) : null}

So in Firefox or Safari the button is not rendered, and every other route to a
colour (the brand swatches, the in-document swatches, the hex field, the opacity
slider) is untouched. That is a graceful degradation by construction; it was NOT
observed in a non-Chromium engine on this host.

── Screen reader: NOT EXERCISED ───────────────────────────────────────
No screen reader was driven. VoiceOver cannot be scripted from a probe without
the same machine-level automation permission WebKit needs, and a synthetic
accessibility-tree dump is not a screen-reader pass. What the probes DO establish
is the tree a screen reader would read: one <main> landmark per page, a bypass
block that moves focus to it, an accessible name on every control (45 on the
merge tool), an alt decision on every <img>, `role="alert"` on error surfaces,
and the full keyboard workflow in scenario M. None of that is a substitute for a
person listening to it.
